{# A page that is prose rather than rows.

   Two families use it. /about is the repository README rendered; /help is five
   articles somebody wrote. They were built far apart and looked it -- the
   README page in Bootstrap's `display-4` and `.lead` straight on the grey
   background, the help pages on a white sheet with an icon and a measure. The
   same kind of page twice, with nothing shared above them but the layout.

   Embedded rather than included, because a page has to be able to fill `body`
   with whatever it has: /about holds already-safe HTML in a variable and an
   article has a template of its own, and a block inside a plain `{% include %}`
   cannot be overridden by the page including it. Same reason the search
   sidebar's sections are embedded.

   `note` is for a line that belongs to the document -- where the README came
   from -- and `aside` for a panel beside it, which is where an article puts its
   siblings. Both are empty unless a page fills them.

   `hero` is opt-in and off by default, which is not timidity: five templates
   embed this, and /about is a rendered README whose heading belongs on the
   sheet with the prose rather than over a band. Help and legal pass it; the
   two /about pages pass nothing and are unchanged. #}
{# The heading, captured so it can be printed in one of two places without
   being written twice. `{% set %}` keeps the markup as markup and still
   escapes what is interpolated into it, and the class stays spelled exactly
   `article__title` -- LegalRenderTest matches that with its closing quote, so
   a modifier alongside it would fail the legal suite without changing a
   pixel.

   No icon in a band. One glyph over a centred title reads as decoration
   rather than as a label, and the band already carries the title, the path
   above it and a colour of its own. The sheet keeps its icon, on the baseline
   beside the title, where /about is now the only page drawing one -- and
   `icon` is still read by the hub's cards and the aside's chips, which are
   lists an icon genuinely helps somebody scan. #}
{% set heading %}
    <h1 class="article__title">
        {%- if not hero|default(false) %}
            <i class="fas {{ icon }} article__title-icon" aria-hidden="true"></i>
        {% endif %}
        {{ title }}
    </h1>
{% endset %}

<main role="main" class="article{{ hero|default(false) ? ' article--hero' : '' }}">
    {% if hero|default(false) %}
        {# The band. Not called `hero`: main.css makes the header sticky on any
           page containing `.hero`, matched on the element rather than a page
           class, so borrowing the name would change the header's behaviour on
           every article. `:has(.hero)` is a class selector and not a prefix,
           so `article__hero` does not match it.

           The trail comes inside, where it does the job the reference puts an
           "all articles" eyebrow to -- and where it has to be, since the strip
           it normally sits in would otherwise show a band of page background
           between the header and this. #}
        <div class="article__hero">
            <div class="container">
                {% include 'partials/breadcrumbs.html.twig' with {'on_dark': true, 'hide_current': true} %}
                {{ heading }}
            </div>
        </div>
    {% endif %}

    <div class="container article__layout">
        <div class="article__sheet">
            {% if not hero|default(false) %}
                {{ heading }}
            {% endif %}

            {# The sentence the page is about, set apart from the answer rather
               than run into it. On an article it is also the meta description,
               so somebody arriving from a search result reads the sentence that
               brought them. #}
            {% if lead is defined and lead is not empty %}
                <p class="article__lead">{{ lead }}</p>
            {% endif %}

            {# `body_class` is for prose nobody here wrote. The README arrives as
               generic markdown with no classes to hook, so `.readme` carries
               the levels and the blocks only it has -- code, tables, an image.
               An article needs none of that and passes nothing. #}
            <div class="article__body{{ body_class is defined and body_class ? ' ' ~ body_class : '' }}">
                {% block body %}{% endblock %}
            </div>

            {% block note %}{% endblock %}
        </div>

        {% block aside %}{% endblock %}
    </div>
</main>
